.TH DCH 1 "July 2026" "dch 1.0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dch \- detachable terminal sessions with a headless agent API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dch
.RI [ cmd... ]
.br
.B dch
.RB [ \-l | \-ls | \-lj | \-rl | \-k
.RI [ name ]
.RB | \-kl | \-d
.RI [ name ]]
.br
.B dch \-\-spawn
.I name
.RB [ \-\-size
.IR CxR ]
.RI [ cmd... ]
.br
.B dch \-\-send\fR|\fB\-\-run\fR|\fB\-\-keys
.I name args...
.br
.B dch \-\-read
.I name
.RB [ \-\-ansi ]
.RB [ \-\-cursor ]
.RB [ \-\-recent
.RI [ N ]]
.br
.B dch \-\-wait
.I name
.B \-\-match
.I str
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR ms ]
.br
.B dch \-\-say
.RI [ name... ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.B \-\-
.I text...
.br
.B dch \-\-stop
.RI [ name... ]
.RB [ \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B dch \-\-restart
.IR name \fR|\fP \fB\-\-all\fP
.RB [ \-\-include\-agents ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B dch \-\-revive
.IR name \fR|\fP \fB\-\-all\fP
.RB [ \-\-as
.IR new ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.BR "dch \-\-pin" | \-\-unpin
.I name...
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B dch \-\-forget
.IR name... " [" \fB\-f\fP "]|" \fB\-\-all\fP
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B dch \-\-agent\-list
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B dch \-\-agent\-send
.I peer message...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dch
keeps a program running in a detachable session (a fork of dtach). Run bare,
it attaches to the auto-named session for the current directory
(\fI<repo>\-<branch>\fR or the cwd basename), creating it if needed. The
session master survives terminal crashes, GUI quits, and SSH drops; a new
.B dch
in the same directory reattaches. Detach with
.BR Ctrl\-\(rs .
.PP
If the socket node itself is deleted while the master is alive \(em some
external cleanup, not
.BR dch 's
own doing \(em the master notices on its next idle tick (hourly by default;
.B DCH_HEAL_MS
overrides it) and re\-binds the socket under the same name, re\-stamping the
version sidecar and refreshing the activity/state sidecars. Left alone, a
missing socket node is invisible to
.B \-\-status
and
.BR \-\-ls\-json ,
and the next attach starts a
.I second
master under the same name instead of finding the first. The heal never
touches a socket node that is present, so it cannot recover a
name a second master has already taken; see
.B DCH_SOCKET_DIR
below for avoiding the underlying cause.
.PP
Each session also keeps an in-memory terminal mirror (embedded
libghostty\-vt), which powers the control verbs below: scripts and coding
agents can type into, read, and wait on a session without attaching and
without disturbing an attached human.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-l
Pick a session interactively and attach. A live mirror preview of each
session is shown while picking.
When a session runs a named Claude Code or Codex session, that name is shown
as \fIdisplay-name (real-name)\fP. Claude names are read from
.IR ~/.claude/sessions ;
Codex titles from
.IR ~/.codex/session_index.jsonl .
Current Codex thread IDs come from matching shell-snapshot filenames; older
process-environment IDs remain supported. Both are joined only through a live
harness process's
.B DCH_SESSION
so stale records do not overlay; explicit aliases win.
.TP
.B \-ls
List sessions one per line as \fIdisplay-name (real-name)\fP when an alias or
harness title exists, otherwise as \fIreal-name\fP. Aliases win over harness
titles.
.TP
.B \-lj
List sessions as \fIname\fP<TAB>\fIalias\fP<TAB>\fIactivity_epoch\fP lines.
.TP
.B \-\-ls\-json
List sessions as a JSON array of
.BR {name,alias,harness,activity_epoch,state,version} .
The
.I harness
field carries the name of the Claude Code or Codex session running inside,
when one is; the real
.I name
and display
.I alias
remain separate fields.
.TP
.B \-rl
Rename (alias) sessions interactively. Aliases are display-only.
.TP
.BI \-m " name " [ alias ]
Set or clear an alias non-interactively.
.TP
.BR \-k " [\fIname\fP]"
Kill a session (interactive picker without a name).
.TP
.B \-kl
Kill all sessions.
.TP
.BR \-d " [\fIname\fP]"
Detach all clients of a session.
.TP
.BI \-n " name"
Override the auto-name.
.TP
.B \-f
Force-attach even if another client is connected.
.TP
.BI \-e " char"
Set the detach character (default
.BR ^\(rs ).
.TP
.B \-E
Disable the detach character.
.SH SWITCHING SESSIONS
Pressing the detach character
.B twice
within the double\-press window detaches and opens the same interactive picker as
.BR "dch \-l" ,
so you can move straight into another session. Quitting the picker leaves you
detached. A single press still detaches; it simply waits out the window first.
.PP
.B DCH_DOUBLE_TAP_MS
sets that window in milliseconds (default
.BR 300 ).
Setting it to
.B 0
disables switching and makes detach instant again.
.SH CONTROL VERBS
All verbs take the session
.I name
first, never attach, and are safe to run while a human is attached.
.TP
.BI \-\-spawn " name" " " [ \-\-size " CxR" ] " " [ cmd... ]
Start a headless session running
.I cmd
(default:
.BR $SHELL ).
Size defaults to
.BR $COLUMNS x $LINES ,
then 80x24. Prints the session name. Exits 1 if the session exists or
.I cmd
cannot be executed (the error is printed); a
.I cmd
that runs and exits at once is still a successful spawn.
.TP
.BI \-\-send " name text..."
Type
.I text
(arguments joined with spaces) into the session, no enter.
.TP
.BI \-\-run " name text..."
Same as
.B \-\-send
plus a carriage return.
.TP
.BI \-\-keys " name key..."
Encode and send keys mode-aware (kitty keyboard protocol, application cursor
keys) using the mirror's live terminal state. Key names:
.BR enter ", " esc ", " tab ", " space ", " backspace ", " delete ,
.BR up / down / left / right ", " home ", " end ", " pgup ", " pgdn ,
.BR f1 \(en f12 ,
single characters, with
.BR ctrl+ ", " alt+ ", " shift+
prefixes (e.g.
.BR ctrl+c ", " shift+tab ).
.TP
.BI \-\-read " name" " " [ \-\-ansi ] " " [ \-\-cursor ] " " [ \-\-recent " [\fIN\fP]]"
Print the session's rendered screen to stdout.
.B \-\-ansi
keeps colors/attributes \(em including the faint attribute and the exact
foreground color, which is the only thing that separates a TUI's own ghost
text (placeholders, argument hints) from characters the human typed. The plain
dump drops that, so a watcher deciding whether the prompt box holds an unsent
message must read with
.B \-\-ansi
.RB and " \-\-cursor" ;
see
.I docs/ghost\-text.md
in the source tree.
.B \-\-recent
prints the last
.I N
lines including scrollback (default 100).
.B \-\-cursor
additionally prints
.RI \(lqcursor " row col visible wrap" \(rq
to
.IR stderr ,
taken from the same snapshot as the screen on stdout, so a caller painting
that screen knows where the caret is instead of inferring it.
.I row
and
.I col
are 1\-based like
.BR CUP ,
with the row counted from the top of the visible screen;
.I visible
is DEC mode 25 (0 = hidden);
.I wrap
is 1 when
.I col
is the last column and the
.I next
printed character soft\-wraps to the next row \(em the caret is still on
.IR col ,
not past it.
.I row
counts screen rows while the screen dump has its trailing blank lines
trimmed, so a caret parked below the last line of content reports a
.I row
past the end of the dump; leading and interior blank rows are kept, so row
.I N
is line
.I N
of the dump whenever the dump reaches that far. Not valid with
.BR \-\-recent
(the caret belongs to the visible screen, not the scrollback tail). Exit 0 on
success, 3 if the session has no mirror, 1 if
.B \-\-cursor
was asked of a session master that predates it (restart the session) or of a
screen so large the response was truncated (the caret would land on the wrong
row of the kept tail, so it is withheld).
.TP
.BI \-\-wait " name " \-\-match " str" " " [ \-\-timeout " ms" ]
Block until the rendered screen or recent scrollback contains the literal
substring
.I str
(at most 512 bytes); print the matching line. Default timeout 10000 ms.
Exit 0 on match, 2 on timeout, 3 if no mirror.
.TP
.BI \-\-restart " name" "\fR|\fP" \-\-all " " [ \-\-include\-agents ] " " [ \-f ] " " [ \-\-json ]
Re\-execute the session's master process onto the current
.B dch
binary, in place. The program inside the session keeps running: its process,
its pty, its screen and every attached client survive, because the master
replaces its own image with
.BR execv (2)
without forking, carrying its file descriptors across. The target is the
.B dch
binary that ran this command, so a session started from a versioned install
prefix still lands on the newly installed build. Use this after upgrading
.B dch
so long\-lived sessions gain new features without being recreated.
.B \-\-all
restarts every session whose reported version differs from this binary's;
.B \-f
restarts them all regardless. Exit 0 when the new image is serving, 1 if the
re\-exec failed (the old master rolls back and keeps serving) or if the master
is too old to know the verb, in which case the session must be recreated.
Scrollback held only in the mirror is not carried across a restart; the
visible screen is.
.IP
With
.BR \-\-include\-agents ,
the master pass is followed by restarting Claude Code agents: in every session
with
.BR \-\-all ,
in the named session only otherwise. Without it no agent is touched,
.B \-\-all
included.
.B \-\-include\-agents
without
.B \-\-restart
is a usage error. A session qualifies when a live interactive Claude session
record (under
.IR ~/.claude/sessions ,
or
.BR CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR )
belongs to a descendant of the session's child, with a matching process start
time, and Claude is its command (a
.B claude
token, directly or behind a wrapper such as
.BR "headroom wrap claude" ,
with the session's working directory). An agent
is restarted when its record's version differs from what that
.B claude
reports for
.B \-\-version
on the master's
.BR PATH ,
or always with
.BR \-f .
Each such agent is sent the pause message, typed only while its composer is
on screen and empty; Enter follows only once the text (or Claude's
.B [Pasted text #N]
placeholder) shows in the composer row, and the same holds for
.B /exit
and the resume message. All agents are paused first and then waited on
together. An agent is ready when its record reads idle, it has visibly taken
the pause message, no subagents show on its agent panel, and that holds for a
settle window. dch then holds the session, types
.BR /exit ,
takes the session id from the last
.B claude \-\-resume
.I id
line Claude prints, and relaunches the original command with
.B \-\-resume
.I id
inserted after the
.B claude
token. Any
.BR \-\-resume ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-\-continue ,
.BR \-c ,
.B \-\-session\-id
and
.B \-\-fork\-session
in the original are dropped. Once the new Claude is idle, dch types the
resume message and checks the new version. Each agent gets one result line,
and the exit status is 1 if any of them failed. A failed agent is left
running; one that crashed on
.B /exit
is not relaunched; its last output lines go into the report. Skipped: Codex
sessions, a
.B claude
typed into a session's shell
.RB ( in_shell :
.B /exit
would hand the shell back to run whatever followed
.B claude
on its line), a session with a pending respawn, and the session running the
command. A positional prompt argument is sent
again on resume. A dch\-lite client skips this step, with a note when a
session runs claude and
.B \-\-include\-agents
was given. The step can take up to
.BR DCH_AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS ;
from inside Claude Code run it in the background. SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP
types the resume message into agents still waiting to pause and lets
restarts past
.B /exit
finish; a second signal exits at once. SIGKILL leaves a waiting agent paused.
If a second signal lands after Claude has already exited into the hold,
nothing is relaunched and the session ends when
.B DCH_HOLD_MS
runs out (default 120 s). To recover, run
.B claude \-\-resume
.I id
by hand; the id is in the
.B tail
field of
.B dch \-\-cmd
.I session
until the hold ends.
A turn that starts between the last idle check and
.B /exit
can queue the
.BR /exit ;
if that turn outlasts
.BR DCH_AGENT_STEP_MS ,
the hold is cancelled, the agent is reported as not restarted, and the
session ends when the queued
.B /exit
runs.
.IP
.B \-\-json
(with or without
.BR \-\-include\-agents )
makes stdout NDJSON: one
.RI { name ", " phase ": " master ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per session from the master pass
.RI ( result " " ok ", " skipped " or " failed ),
then
.RI { name ", " phase }
lines
.RB ( pausing ", " exiting ", " relaunching ", " resuming )
and one
.RI { name ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per session from the agent step; no summary lines. The shapes and reason
codes are listed in the README under Machine\-readable output.
.TP
.BI \-\-respawn " name " [ \-\- " cmd..." ]
Arm a one\-shot respawn. When the session's program next exits, the master
keeps the session (socket, pty, attached clients) and starts
.I cmd
in its place. With no
.I cmd
the session is held childless until a second
.B \-\-respawn
brings one, or until
.B DCH_HOLD_MS
ends it. An unconsumed arm expires after
.BR DCH_RESPAWN_TTL_MS .
.B \-\-restart
refuses while a respawn is armed or held.
.TP
.BI \-\-respawn " name " \-\-cancel
Drop a pending respawn. A held session ends.
.TP
.BI \-\-cmd " name"
Print one JSON object:
.IR pid " (null while held), " state " (" running ", " armed " or " held ),
.IR argv ,
.I exit_code
and
.I exit_signal
of a child reaped under an arm (otherwise null), and
.IR tail ,
the last 8 KB the child wrote since the arm.
.TP
.BI \-\-say " name..." " " [ \-\-json ] " \-\- text..."
.TQ
.BR "\-\-say \-\-all " [ \-\-json ] " \-\- \fItext...\fP"
Type
.I text
(the words after the required
.BR \-\- ,
joined with spaces) into the Claude Code running in each named session, or
every session with
.BR \-\-all ,
and press Enter. Claude may be the session's command or typed into its
shell. Only an idle Claude (its session record says so) whose screen shows
the empty composer is typed into; a busy Claude, a prompt or dialog, an unsent
draft or a queued message is skipped, never waited for. Enter is pressed again
while the text stays in the composer. Sessions are typed into concurrently.
One line per session as it finishes:
.IB name ": sent" ,
.IB name ": skipped \(em " why
or
.IB name ": failed \(em " why .
.B \-\-json
prints one object per line instead:
.IR name ,
.IR result " (" ok ", " skipped " or " failed ),
.I reason
.RB ( not_claude ", " self ", " busy ", " prompt ", " draft ", " queued ,
.BR no_screen ", " no_session ", " not_submitted ", " not_typed ,
.BR old_master ,
or empty) and
.IR detail .
The caller's own session
.RB ( DCH_SESSION )
is skipped. A dch-lite build has no mirror to check the composer with and
skips every Claude session
.RB ( no_screen ).
Exits 0 when nothing failed, 1 on a usage error or when any session failed.
.TP
.BI \-\-stop " name..." " " [ \-\-json ]
.TQ
.BR "\-\-stop \-\-all " [ \-\-json ]
End sessions so that
.B \-\-revive
can bring them back. A session whose Claude Code is live, as its command or
typed into its shell, is paused first as
.B \-\-include\-agents
pauses it: a pause message and a wait until it is provably idle. Claude as
the command then gets
.B /exit
through the same composer gate, its resume id read from Claude's exit line
(else from its session record). Claude typed into the shell never gets
.BR /exit ,
as the shell would run whatever followed
.B claude
on its line: its resume id is read from its session record and the
session's end hangs up the shell and Claude together.
Then the session ends. A session without a
live Claude ends at once. Its history record ends
.B stopped
and keeps the agent with that id;
.B \-\-revive \-\-all
skips such records and
.BI \-\-revive " name"
wakes one, Claude resumed and told it was stopped and to re\-arm what it
stopped. Sessions are handled concurrently. One line per session as it
finishes:
.IB name ": stopped (claude " "name id" "; revive with dch \-\-revive " name ) ,
.IB name ": skipped \(em " why
.RB ( self ", " respawn_pending ", " no_screen ),
.IB name ": failed \(em " why
.RB ( no_session ", " old_master ", " prompt ", " draft ", " queued ,
.BR not_typed ", " not_idle ", " exit_failed ", " claude_exited ,
.BR end_failed )
or
.IB name ": interrupted \(em " why .
A failure leaves the session running and types a resume message into its
Claude; so does a first SIGINT or SIGTERM for every Claude not yet past
.BR /exit ;
a second one exits at once.
.B \-\-json
prints NDJSON instead:
.RI { name ", " phase }
lines
.RB ( pausing ", " exiting ", " ending ;
no
.B exiting
for Claude typed into the shell)
as each session reaches them, then one
.RI { name ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per session, with
.IR result " " ok ", " skipped ", " failed " or " interrupted .
A dch-lite build skips sessions with a live Claude
.RB ( no_screen );
the rest still stop. Exits 0 when nothing failed, 1 on a usage error or
when any session failed or was interrupted.
.TP
.BI \-\-revive " name" "\fR|\fP" \-\-all " " [ \-\-as " new" ] " " [ \-\-json ]
Start ended sessions again from their history records: in their cwd, with
their argv, with their Claude Code resumed via
.B \-\-resume
.I id
(relaunched as the command, or typed into the revived shell) and told to
carry on.
.B \-\-all
takes the sessions that died in the last 3 days after running 5 minutes or
more. One line per session.
.B \-\-json
prints NDJSON instead:
.RI { name ", " phase }
lines
.RB ( starting ", " resuming )
and one
.RI { name ", " as ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per session, where
.I as
is the name it came back under. Exits 0 when nothing failed, 1 otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-pin " name..." "\fR|\fP" \-\-unpin " name..." " " [ \-\-json ]
Keep a session's history record past the 30-day prune, or stop keeping it.
A pin is an empty
.IB file .pin
beside the record, keyed by the name: a new session under a pinned name is
pinned too, and a revive under another name moves the pin.
.B \-\-pin
needs a record, live or ended
.RB ( not_found );
.B \-\-unpin
always succeeds.
.TP
.BI \-\-forget " name..." " " [ \-f "] [" \-\-json ]
.TQ
.BR "\-\-forget \-\-all " [ \-\-json ]
Delete history records: never a live session's
.RB ( live ),
a pinned one only with
.BR \-f ,
which removes the pin too (else
.BR pinned ).
.B \-\-all
deletes every record neither live nor pinned and ends with
.BI "forgot " N " (" K " pinned, " L " live kept)" .
.B \-\-json
for
.BR \-\-pin ", " \-\-unpin " and " \-\-forget
prints one
.RI { name ", " result ", " reason ", " detail ", " session_id }
per name (per record for
.BR \-\-all ),
then
.RI { ok ", " skipped ", " failed }
counts. Exits 0 when nothing failed, 1 otherwise.
.TP
.BR \-\-agent\-list " [" \-\-json "]"
List live authenticated Claude native peers. The default writes exact names,
one per line. With
.BR \-\-json ,
it writes an array of objects containing
.I name
and
.IR session_id .
Inside a bridged Codex session the sandbox blocks
.B ps
and signals to other processes, so liveness there means only that the
recorded pid still exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-agent\-send " peer message..."
Send a message from the current bridged Codex session to one exact peer name.
Arguments after the name are joined with spaces. The command prints
.B sent
and exits 0 once the authenticated frame has been written to the peer's
socket; it does not wait for a receipt, because Claude sends none for
messages it accepts. Ambiguous, unknown, or unreachable peers exit 1. The
source sidecar remains available after this command exits,
so the peer can reply later.
.PP
dch registers a session whose command runs
.B codex
(directly or through a wrapper such as
.BR "headroom wrap codex" )
as a Claude native peer. The peer name is the Codex thread title when the
thread has one, otherwise the
.B DCH_SESSION
name; the title is re-read every five seconds.
Registration starts after Codex writes the first shell snapshot and only when
the launch marker identifies one UUID. Multiple matching UUIDs are ambiguous;
start a fresh dch Codex session instead of risking delivery to the wrong
thread. Sessions started by older dch versions have no marker and do not
register. A native message is typed into the Codex composer through the
session master as one bracketed paste followed by Enter, so a running turn
steers it in after the current tool call. dch types only while the screen
shows the empty composer placeholder; an approval prompt, form, or pager holds
the message for up to ten minutes, a draft or an unknown screen for ten
seconds. After that, and always without the terminal mirror, delivery falls
back to
.B codex queue,
which the TUI submits at the end of the current turn. If the bound thread has
no rollout yet, the queue path retains up to 16 messages in FIFO order and
retries every five seconds. This does not start the first turn in an unused
TUI. Accepted and waiting messages produce no receipt, matching Claude's own
behaviour; only a lost message does (refused, or dropped). An orderly restart
or shutdown attempts dropped receipts for pending messages; a crash or SIGKILL
is not durable. Messages are limited to 16384 bytes and wire frames to 65536
bytes. The bridge handles one message at a time and rejects attachments.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. Control verbs: 1 generic error, 2
.B \-\-wait
timeout, 3 unavailable in this build. A dch-lite build has no terminal mirror;
native agent commands are available in both builds.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DCH_SOCKET_DIR
Session socket directory (default
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dch\-$UID ,
falling back to
.I /tmp/dch\-$UID
when
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset \(em the common case on macOS). For a session meant to sit idle for
days, point this somewhere other than
.I /tmp
so a periodic cleaner never has a reason to touch it;
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
itself is honored the same way when set.
.TP
.B DCH_HEAL_MS
Self\-heal tick interval in milliseconds (default 3600000, i.e. hourly); see
.B DESCRIPTION
above. Mainly a test knob for shortening the interval.
.TP
.B DCH_NO_VT
If set when a session is spawned, disable its terminal mirror.
.TP
.B DCH_SCROLLBACK
Mirror scrollback lines (default 2000, max 100000).
.TP
.B DCH_NO_REPLAY
If set when a session is spawned, do not replay the mirrored screen to
attaching clients. Attach then behaves as it did before: the client's screen
is cleared and the program is asked to repaint itself, which diff\-based
renderers do not do. Escape hatch only.
.TP
.B DCH_HOLD_MS
Read by the master: how long a held (childless) session waits for its
next command before ending (default 120000).
.TP
.B DCH_RESPAWN_TTL_MS
Read by the master: how long an unconsumed respawn arm lives (default 300000).
.TP
.B DCH_PAUSE_MSG
Text
.B "\-\-restart \-\-include\-agents"
or
.B \-\-stop
types to pause a Claude agent (default begins
.I "pause all work, i need to restart you"
or
.IR "pause all work, this session is being stopped" ).
.TP
.B DCH_RESUME_MSG
Text typed into a relaunched Claude agent, or one
.B \-\-stop
gives up on (default begins
.IR "resume all work" ).
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_TIMEOUT_MS
How long an agent may take to pause (default 600000), for
.B \-\-include\-agents
and
.BR \-\-stop .
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_SETTLE_MS
How long an agent must stay ready before
.B /exit
(default 3000).
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_LAND_MS
How long dch waits for typed text to show in the composer before giving up
without pressing Enter (default 3000).
.TP
.B DCH_AGENT_STEP_MS
How long
.B /exit
may take to end Claude, and the relaunch to show its composer (default
60000).
.TP
.B DCH_DEBUG
Trace to a file (path, or 1 for
.IR /tmp/dch.<uid>.trace ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dtach (1),
.BR screen (1),
.BR tmux (1)
